3 The kanji list
****************

‘M-x heisig-gt-kanji’ opens it.  With a prefix argument it reads a
character and puts point on its row; ‘C-u M-x heisig-gt-kanji RET 目’
goes straight to 目.  When that character has no row the point is left
where it was and a message says why — the database has no entry for it,
or the active filters exclude it, which are different problems and call
for different answers.

   Each row is one kanji, or one of Heisig's primitives — the elements
such as ‘[apron]’ that the book names but Unicode does not encode as
characters of their own.

Key     Does
-----------------------------------------------------
‘RET’   show the kanji at point in detail
‘TAB’   the same, in the other window
‘w’     list the words written with it
‘M-w’   copy the character to the kill ring
‘/’     add a filter (‘C-u /’ to negate it)
‘D’     remove one filter
‘c’     show or hide one column
‘C’     choose the column set and its order
‘s’     sort by the next sortable column
‘V’     reset the view
‘g’     re-read the database and Anki, then redraw
‘q’     quit

