3.3 Readings
============

All four reading columns are on by default: ‘onyomi’ and ‘kunyomi’ in
kana, ‘onyomi-romaji’ and ‘kunyomi-romaji’ transliterated beside them.
That is a wide default; ‘c’ drops whichever pair you do not want.

   ‘by-onyomi’ and ‘by-kunyomi’ accept a reading in either script and
complete on both, so ‘kou’ and ‘コウ’ select the same rows and neither
needs an input method.  Typing romaji at the prompt is the reason the
transliteration is stored at all.

   ‘by-onyomi-romaji’ and ‘by-kunyomi-romaji’ take a *regexp* over the
transliteration instead, for when the fragment is what you know rather
than the whole reading.  ‘by-onyomi’ with ‘kou’ selects the kanji read
exactly that; ‘by-onyomi-romaji’ with ‘^ko’ selects every kanji with an
on reading beginning that way, and with ‘ou$’ every one ending in a long
o.  The two kinds of reading stay apart either way: an on reading never
matches a kun filter.

   The romaji is written by the builder, not derived as a row is drawn:
it is the same answer every time, and a list of 2000 kanji would
otherwise transliterate every reading on every redraw.  It is spelled
the way an IME expects rather than in strict Hepburn — plain ASCII, no
macrons, ‘shi’ rather than ‘si’, ‘jou’ rather than ‘jō’ — because it
exists to be typed.  The okurigana dot and the prefix and suffix dashes
are dropped from it, so ‘むずか.しい’ is ‘muzukashii’; the kana column
keeps them.  A reading ending in a small tsu becomes ‘t’, so ‘ハッ’ is
‘hat’ and does not collapse onto ‘ハ’.


