3.4 Saving a view
=================

‘M-x bookmark-set’ stores the view: the displayed columns, the active
filters, the sort key, and — in a word buffer — its seed.  Jumping to it
restores them and re-reads the data.

   Set ‘heisig-gt-kanji-persist-state’ to have the view survive between
sessions; it is written to ‘heisig-gt-kanji-state-file’, one entry per
list.  Both stores keep the same view, so a view restored from a file
and the same view restored from a bookmark are the same view.


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: Component names,  Prev: Saving a view,  Up: The kanji list

3.5 Component names
===================

The ‘components’ column shows what a character is built from; the
‘component-keywords’ column names those components in English, so 早
reads ‘日十’ in one and ‘day, ten’ in the other.

   Only the Academy source glosses components, so a third of them carry
no name of their own.  Where one does not, the component is usually a
character with a row and a keyword of its own — 丿 is a component of 乏
and a kanji keyworded ‘katana’ — and that keyword names it; failing
that, the first clause of whatever dictionary gloss was recorded does.
Between them these name all but two of the 4221 components in the
database.  A component nothing names is left out rather than shown as a
gap, so the names line up with each other rather than with the
characters.

   Two glosses in ‘kanji-all.json’ are the literal text ‘None’ — a null
that something stringified on its way into the file.  The builder treats
those, and ‘null’, ‘nan’ and ‘undefined’, as absent, so no component is
named ‘None’ and no filter offers it.


