4 The word list
***************

‘M-x heisig-gt-words’ lists Japanese words rather than kanji, ordered by
how often they occur in Netflix subtitles.  From the kanji list or the
detail buffer, ‘w’ opens it seeded with the kanji at point.  Going back
the other way, ‘k’ opens the detail of one of the word's kanji and ‘K’
goes to its row in the kanji list; both ask which kanji when the word is
written with more than one.

   A word buffer has a *seed* — the characters whose words were loaded —
and filters that narrow what the seed brought in.  The mode line names
the seed first, then the filters, then the counts: ‘[目] [kanji=的]
1/82’.

Key     Does
---------------------------------------------------
‘k’     show the detail of a kanji of the word
‘RET’   the same
‘K’     go to that kanji's row in the kanji list
‘S’     reseed (replaces the seed; empty = all)
‘M-w’   copy the word to the kill ring

   Columns, filtering, sorting, bookmarks and saved views work exactly
as they do in the kanji list — it is the same mode underneath, with its
own column and filter registries.  Columns: ‘rank’, ‘root’, ‘surface’,
‘lemma’, ‘reading’, ‘romaji’, ‘pos’, ‘unidic-pos’, ‘jlpt’, ‘count’,
‘pct’, ‘cum-pct’, ‘definition’.  Filters: ‘by-kanji’, ‘by-romaji’,
‘by-reading’, ‘by-pos’, ‘by-jlpt’, ‘by-rank’, ‘by-definition-regexp’.

   Seeding widens and filtering narrows, the same rule the kanji list
follows: a seed of 目川 loads every word using *either*, and then adding
a ‘by-kanji’ filter for 的 keeps only those using *that as well*.

   Any character counts, not only kanji — seeding with ‘メ’ finds カメラ
and メール — and the seed and the ‘by-kanji’ filter always agree,
because both read one set of characters computed per word when the
database was built.

   ‘by-romaji’ searches the pronunciation without an input method, and
is a regexp rather than a completion: a word's reading is nearly unique,
so choosing one from a list would only ever select that word, while
‘kou’ usefully selects hundreds.

