4.1 How much to load
====================

‘heisig-gt-words-max-rank’ (default 20000) is how far down the order the
list reaches.

   The database holds every word that resolved to a dictionary headword
— some 52000 — rather than a common few thousand, because a fifth of the
frequency report is not really vocabulary (stray katakana, initialisms,
names the tokeniser kept) and where that junk sits decides what any
given cut-off actually contains.  Raising the ceiling needs no rebuild.

   For scale, by rank:

words   of all speech
------------------------
1000    82%
5000    93%
12000   96%
20000   98%


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: Where the words come from,  Prev: How much to load,  Up: The word list

4.2 Where the words come from
=============================

The list and its order come from a UniDic-tokenised frequency report
over Japanese Netflix subtitles.  Readings, parts of speech and
definitions come from the dictionary ‘yomikun-gt’ ships; the JLPT level,
where there is one, from its N5, N4 and N3 vocabulary lists — those
three exist, so a word above N3 has no level rather than a guessed one.

   Three things the builder does that are worth knowing:

   • One word occupies several lines of the report, one per spelling the
     tokeniser saw: 言う is counted separately as 言う, いう and 言える.
     They are merged on the headword and reading, summing the counts, so
     a word ranks by how often it was *said* rather than by how often it
     was said spelled one particular way.
   • UniDic writes disambiguated lemmas — ‘私-代名詞’, ‘ママ-mama’ —
     which no dictionary headword carries.  The bare lemma, the
     on-screen spelling and the reading are each tried in turn.
   • Every value in that dictionary was written by emacsql, which wraps
     each one in literal double quotes: ‘を’ is stored as ‘"を"’.  They
     are stripped once, at build time.

   Cumulative coverage is measured against the whole corpus, not against
what was imported, so it stops short of 100% by however much the dropped
entries were worth.


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: The detail buffer,  Next: Study progress,  Prev: The word list,  Up: Top

5 The detail buffer
*******************

‘RET’ in the list, or ‘M-x heisig-gt-detail-show’, fills one buffer with
everything the database holds about a single kanji.  One buffer is
reused, so walking the components leaves one window in place rather than
a trail of them.

Key     Does
----------------------------------------------
‘RET’   follow the component at point
‘n’     next Heisig frame
‘p’     previous Heisig frame
‘w’     list the words written with it
‘k’     go to its row in the kanji list
‘l’     the same
‘M-w’   copy the character to the kill ring
‘g’     redraw
‘q’     quit

   Every character shown as a component, or as one of the characters the
current one is a component of, is a button.  A character the database
has no entry for is shown dimmed and is not one: the component graph
reaches outside the decks the database was built from, and a button that
led nowhere would be worse than none.


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: Study progress,  Next: Where the data comes from,  Prev: The detail buffer,  Up: Top

6 Study progress
****************

Progress is read from the Anki collection on every ‘g’, not copied into
the database when it is built: intervals change daily, and a column
showing yesterday's would be worse than no column.

   ‘heisig-gt-anki-file’ is the collection, and ‘heisig-gt-anki-decks’
the decks whose cards describe a kanji.  Subdecks are included, and a
card in a filtered deck counts towards the deck it came from.  A kanji
with more than one card is described by the one furthest along.

   Anki's queues map onto five statuses: ‘new’, ‘learning’, ‘review’,
‘suspended’ and ‘buried’.  ‘by-status’ also offers ‘none’, for the kanji
Anki does not have at all.

   A collection that is missing, unreadable, or not an Anki database is
never an error.  The study columns are empty, the mode line says why,
and everything else works unchanged.


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: Where the data comes from,  Next: Development,  Prev: Study progress,  Up: Top

7 Where the data comes from
***************************

‘tools/build-heisig-db.py’ merges three sources, preferring them in this
order per field:

  1. The Anki deck ‘jp::kanjiMigaku’, whose notes carry a base64-encoded
     ‘MigakuData’ field.  This is the only source with Heisig frame
     numbers, Heisig's own stories, the primitive graph, and any keyword
     you overrode yourself.
  2. The Anki deck ‘jp::kanjiAcademy’, which adds component glosses, a
     mnemonic sentence per kanji, and compound words.
  3. ‘kanji-all.json’.

   Every row records which of the three it drew on, in the ‘sources’
column and the ‘by-source’ filter.  That matters, because the JSON
source has had every space stripped from its English text: it reads
‘oncemore,again’ where it should read ‘once more, again’.  It is used
only where nothing better exists, and where it was used it says so.

   Frequency ranks of 999999 — the sentinel both Anki sources use for
"unranked" — are stored as unknown rather than as the rarest rank there
is.


File: heisig-gt.info,  Node: Development,  Next: License,  Prev: Where the data comes from,  Up: Top

8 Development
*************

     make               # byte-compile
     make test          # the ERT suite
     make check         # compile + lint + checkdoc + check-declare + test
     make check-ci      # the same, under every Emacs the CI matrix pins
     make db            # build the kanji database

   The suite reads fixtures it generates: a miniature Anki collection
from ‘tools/make-test-fixture.py’, merged with
‘test/fixtures/mini-kanji.json’ by the same builder the user runs.  A
schema the reader and the builder disagree about therefore fails the
tests rather than surfacing later against real data.  No test touches
your own database or collection.

   Run ‘make check-ci’ before pushing: ‘make check’ runs under whatever
‘emacs’ is on PATH, which cannot prove the package works on the versions
CI pins.


